-> What is an inline function?

In a normal function call, arguments are pushed onto the stack and control is transferred to the function, which takes time.
When a function is small and simple, it can be declared as inline. So here, function call is replaced by the actual function code, 
which saves stack operations and execution time

An inline function is a function in which the function call is replaced by its actual code to reduce function call overhead and improve performance

inline int sum(int a, int b){
    return a + b;
}

int main(){
    sum(2, 3);   //it will directly replaced by 2 + 3; saving function call time
}

// What actually happens in Back-Ground when function is call
The values you pass to the function are stored in memory (stack) {Arguments are pushed onto the stack}
The program stores the location (address) where it should return after the function finishes
Execution moves from the calling point to the function definition
The function runs its code
The result is returned to the calling location
Arguments and local variables are removed from the stack {Stack is cleaned}

Why this takes time
All these steps involve:
Stack operations
Memory access
Jumping between locations
