-> What is the difference between deep copy and shallow copy?

In Shallow copy, it copies the memory address, therfore change in data in first variable also cause change in second variable
whereas in Deep copy, it directly copies Actual data, therfore change in first variable doesnt cause change in Second Variable

In shallow copy, addresses are copied, so both objects share the same memory and changes affect both.
In deep copy, separate memory is allocated and actual data is copied, so changes do not affect other objects.

Shallow Copy
int main() {
    int a = 5;
    int *p1 = &a;
    int *p2 = p1;  // shallow copy (just copies the address)

    *p2 = 10;  // change via p2

    cout << "a = " << a << endl;  // a is also changed
    return 0;
}

Deep Copy
int main() {
    int a = 5;
    int *p1 = new int(a);
    int *p2 = new int(*p1);  // deep copy (copies actual value)

    *p2 = 10;  // change p2

    cout << "*p1 = " << *p1 << endl;  // p1 remains unchanged
    cout << "*p2 = " << *p2 << endl;
    
    delete p1;
    delete p2;
    return 0;
}
