-> Can we have multiple catch blocks in C++?

Yes ✅, C++ allows multiple catch blocks for a single try. To handle different types of exceptions differently.
A generic catch block (catch(...)) handles any type of exception.

#include <iostream>
using namespace std;
int main() {
    try {
        throw 10;   // try changing to "error" or 3.14
    }
    catch (int e) {
        cout << "Integer exception: " << e;
    }
    catch (const char* msg) {
        cout << "String exception: " << msg;
    }
    catch (double d) {
        cout << "Double exception: " << d;
    }
}
-----------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

-> What happens if an exception is not caught?

If an exception is not caught, C++ calls std::terminate() and the program ends suddenly and unexpectedly, without any warning or proper completion.
If no catch block handles the exception, the program terminates abnormally.
Example (Unhandled Exception):
int main() {
 throw 5; // No catch block
 return 0;
}
 Result: Program crashes with terminate called after throwing an instance of 'int'
